# Build context for Dockerfile.arm64 ONLY.
#
# BuildKit looks for `<dockerfile>.dockerignore` before falling back to
# `.dockerignore`, so `docker build -f Dockerfile.arm64` reads this file and the
# Cloud Run build keeps reading the other one. That separation is the point:
# the two hosts want opposite answers about chroma_db/, and editing the shared
# .dockerignore to suit Oracle would silently ship Cloud Run an empty index --
# the exact failure its own comment warns about. This way the rollback path
# stays intact until you delete it deliberately.

# THE DIFFERENCE FROM .dockerignore: chroma_db/ IS excluded here.
#
# It is 553MB, it belongs on the mounted volume, and keeping it out of the
# context is most of why a code deploy on this host is seconds rather than
# minutes. Dockerfile.arm64 sets CHROMA_DIR=/data/chroma_db to match.
chroma_db/

# --- everything below is identical to .dockerignore ---

# 6.1GB, and a Windows virtualenv is useless in a Linux image regardless.
.venv/
venv/
env/

# 71MB of concatenated Yelp reviews, an input to the one-time seed scripts.
tagged_reviews.txt
data/
*.parquet
*.csv
*.json

# Secrets never belong in an image layer. Config comes from the environment.
.env
.env.*
env.yaml

# Notebooks, git history and IDE state are not needed to serve requests.
*.ipynb
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.git/
.gitignore
.idea/
.vscode/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# A stray package.json pulled these in; nothing Python uses them.
node_modules/
package-lock.json

# The deploy directory itself -- compose files and Caddyfile are read from the
# host, not from inside the image.
deploy/
